"""Render manually authored close-reading decisions for contribution groups 14–18.

No keyword scoring or automatic semantic decisions. Source IDs/locators refer to
the retained extraction manifest. Reruns overwrite this review's drafts only.
"""
import json
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
BASE = ROOT / 'raw/practitioner-practices-development/extraction'
M = json.loads((BASE/'manifest.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
S = {r['id']: r for r in M['sources']}

# id: author; locators; role; contribution; reported; proposed; limitations.
DATA = {
435: ('Sam Illingworth','L30–44, L54–66, L82–86','origin','Ask whose situation a neutral-sounding answer omits; check a real source or person before revising it.','Worked student-description example; author identifies omitted mature and part-time students.','Broaden represented perspectives.','An omission can follow the prompt or length constraint; no measured fairness improvement. Demographic numbers not independently verified.'),
491: ('Sam Illingworth','L20–44, L58–82','support','Compare a compressed historical answer with absent perspectives; supports the missing-voices exercise.','Shows a Gemini answer and the author’s interpretation of omissions.','Notice historical representation gaps.','The answer includes class conflict and radical politics. Its omissions do not establish training-data causation; the 100-word prompt is a confound. Same author as 435, not independent replication.'),
802: ('Shaun Clowes','L104–159; 00:09:33–00:21:11','origin','Supply actual customer interviews and a strategy; ask where they conflict, then return to those customers and records.','Describes personal prompting and Confluent feedback aggregation.','Find contrary evidence and strategic gaps.','No comparative accuracy or decision-outcome test. Competitor strategy inferred from public documents remains a hypothesis. Fixed interview sample-size advice and more-data-is-always-better claims excluded.'),
760: ('Nicole Forsgren','L377–423; 00:48:54–00:57:35','origin','Review whether each survey question supports a useful decision; separate bundled questions with a researcher or AI critique.','Explains why a build-and-test/slow-or-complicated item is ambiguous.','Make survey responses interpretable.','Expert advice and worked example, not a test of AI survey design. A model calling an item good is not validation. Productivity attribution remains confounded by concurrent changes.'),
591: ('Christian Idiodi','L122–168; 00:14:52–00:24:53','support','Work with people who actually have the problem; distinguish use and expressed approval from value and adoption.','Reports repeated product work and personal success using reference customers.','Ground decisions in lived problems and observed use.','Success is retrospective self-report. Recommended customer counts are heuristics, not proof of product-market fit. Vaccine analogy and unspecified validation studies excluded.'),
421: ('Sam Illingworth','L81–120, L140–146','origin','State tools, tasks, human checks and responsibility; apply disclosure expectations to the people writing the policy too.','Provides his own AI-use statement and a proposed reciprocal classroom policy.','Make responsibility and assistance visible.','No evaluation of the policy or its effects. Disclosure can carry social costs; do not promise trust gains. News, survey figures and tool-version claims are not verified here.'),
529: ('Sam Illingworth','L83–108, L134–160','origin','Keep version history, rough drafts, inputs, research trail and dated AI acceptance/rejection notes while working.','Describes his own speaking, editing and research workflow.','Make the creation process inspectable.','Records are evidence, not conclusive proof of authorship or guaranteed defense against accusation. News cases, detector statistics and contract claims excluded from the practice; no legal advice inferred.'),
819: ('Tomer Cohen','L279–310; 00:35:38–00:42:35','origin','Have product owners explain an algorithm’s objective, inputs and data strategy with the people building it.','Describes LinkedIn product reviews and internal AI training.','Keep product responsibility with people who understand the system.','Corporate first-person account; no measured sovereignty or learning effect. Ranking-system objectives do not transfer unchanged to every generative assistant. Verify actual implementation with engineering.'),
436: ('Sam Illingworth','L43–73, L81–111','origin','Ask what a workplace tool collects, who can access it, where it goes and how it affects assessment.','Uses a fictional onboarding game to illustrate questions.','Make disclosure choices more informed.','Fiction is not a test of named vendors. Blanket surveillance claims not established. The chatbot cannot certify actual telemetry or policy. Unknown answers do not establish malicious intent.'),
440: ('Sam Illingworth','L91–123','origin','Keep dated notes of human interventions: errors caught, escalations and contextual advice.','Proposes a private one-line intervention log.','Make otherwise invisible human work inspectable.','No outcome evaluation. Avoid treating estimated avoided costs as measured savings. News examples and hiring-causation claims excluded. Keep confidential details within authorized systems.'),
482: ('Sam Illingworth','L30–64, L76–90','support','Use invented trivial disclosures to notice how the framing of a privacy question changes generated stories.','Author reports a playful exchange about toast and routines.','Prompt reflection before sharing information.','Not a privacy risk assessment, training of confidentiality, or change to retention settings. Exaggerated and minimized stories can both mislead. No separate recommended privacy card.'),
363: ('Sabrina Ramonov','L33–79','counterexample','A chain asks AI to infer the user’s true identity and insecurities, predict their future, then prescribe action.','Prompt sequence only; no outcome evidence.','Self-discovery and action, as claimed by author.','Caveat that answers are imperfect does not ground psychological claims. Conversation history is partial; emotionally forceful language is not evidence of insight. Do not recommend this chain.'),
602: ('Dan Shipper','L450–490; 00:35:42–00:39:21','counterexample','Reports grades rising from B+ to A across turns, then claims a newer model has better taste.','Personal grading observation and a product-development account.','Useful automatic writing evaluation, as claimed by guest.','No controlled comparison or independent reader outcomes. Do not generalize either grade inflation or claimed repair to all models. Product promotion and founder interest matter.'),
486: ('Sam Illingworth','L28–47, L53–85, L97–102','counterexample','Asks a model to infer what the user is not saying; interprets a responsive exchange as shared understanding.','Author reports an emotionally meaningful conversation.','Reflection and creative expression.','A meaningful experience does not establish access to hidden motives. Creative reader-response hypotheses would require reframing and external checks, not adoption of the original mind-reading premise.'),
441: ('Sam Illingworth','L36–48, L60–86, L102–108','counterexample','Asks a model why it sounded kind, then interprets that explanation as revealing retention design.','Shows a warm reply, generated explanation and the author’s reaction.','Recognize emotionally persuasive output.','Generated self-explanation does not establish optimization goals or internal mechanisms. Warmth experiments concern particular interventions, not proof of this product’s motive. Felt comfort remains a valid report of experience.'),
452: ('Stephen D. Carver, guest contributor hosted by Sam Illingworth','L14, L36–44, L58–106','counterexample','Uses an AI self-description to explain what users misunderstand about AI.','Worked generated explanation and the guest’s interpretation.','Improve AI literacy.','Self-description is not implementation documentation. Statements about retrieval and memory vary by tool configuration. Guest authorship must not be credited to the publication host.'),
478: ('Farida Khalaf, guest contributor hosted by Sam Illingworth','L36–44, L66–76, L100–108','counterexample','Prompts a perceptive ally to infer hidden imposter feelings and supply a reframe, proof and next step.','Khalaf reports a confidence jolt while writing in a second language.','Confidence and overcoming imposter feelings.','Reported confidence is not evidence of skill, accurate self-assessment or clinical benefit. Repeating a statement until it feels true cannot establish its truth. Preserve experience, reject authority claim.'),
487: ('Sam Illingworth','L33–43, L52–63, L77–80','counterexample','Asks AI to guess bodily feeling before supplying the person’s experience.','Model guesses, user corrects it, model adapts.','Notice sensations and develop expressive language.','The exchange shows responsive improvisation, not access to bodily state. A human-first writing exercise would be an editorial redesign, not a validated procedure from this source.'),
399: ('Sabrina Ramonov','L143–201; whole available body','counterexample','Combines a genuinely checkable finish line with the claim that an AI grader’s 9+ score establishes completion.','Author says the newsletter itself used the grading loop.','Autonomous quality improvement.','Word count and presence of fields can be checked; truth, relevance and quality are different. No independently measured productivity or quality advantage. AI-first-for-every-question and ranking claims excluded.'),
299: ('Ruben Hassid','L135–236, L268–298; whole available body','counterexample','Mixes draft-first and retrieval ideas with iterate-until-AI-score-8, purported private-reasoning access and reverse-prompt guessing.','Six proposed workflows and disclosure that AI wrote half the post.','Become smarter while using AI.','The cited studies do not directly test these workflows. Generated reasoning is not guaranteed access to internal computation; scores and guessed prompts do not verify learning or intent. Human-first variants overlap better-bounded sources elsewhere.'),
316: ('Ruben Hassid','L52–190; available body ends at paid-video invitation','counterexample','Curates favorite-author material into a notebook and frames generated advice as a mentor.','Reports preference for source-conditioned advice on a business pitch.','Master topics through personalized advice.','Favorite-author selection is not source-quality review. Felt depth does not establish mastery or accurate advice. Screenshots and linked paid demonstration not inspected; no independent learning check.'),
533: ('Ada Chen Rekhi','L70–107; 00:03:45–00:11:39','origin','Curiosity loops: ask a specific, low-burden question, solicit reasons, choose both domain experts and people who know you, then report back.','Describes collecting podcast-topic feedback from around ten people.','Make advice contextual and surface surprises.','Responses are a convenience sample, not votes establishing the right answer. Her career-question example partly leads despite the stated non-leading principle. Estate-planning and executive-function claims excluded.'),
705: ('Keith Yandell','L215–237; 00:27:23–00:31:08','origin','Ask each side to state strong reasons for the other position; set a decision maker and deadline.','Describes a profitability/growth trade-off at DoorDash.','Improve understanding and resolve disagreement.','Retrospective management account. Agreement or empathy does not prove a decision correct; power differences can suppress disagreement.'),
803: ('Shishir Mehrotra','L342–426; 00:50:43–01:03:42','origin','List questions, then identify the few whose answers change many downstream choices; practice on a fictional teleportation case.','Reports the method in YouTube/Coda discussions and an interview exercise.','Improve question selection and decision framing.','No controlled learning evidence. The two-question limit is a practice constraint, not permission to omit safety or feasibility work in real decisions. Mathematical analogy not treated as a formal result.'),
585: ('Casey Winters','L93–130; 00:06:51–00:16:17','origin','Rehearse a presentation with someone who knows its audience; anticipate their questions and hold real pre-meetings.','Describes coaching at Eventbrite and Pinterest.','Improve preparedness and audience fit.','No comparative meeting outcome data. Simulation cannot establish what a real executive will think; the adviser’s actual familiarity matters.'),
731: ('Matt Abrahams','L648–694; 01:02:57–01:07:16','origin','ADD: answer directly, give a detailed example, describe its relevance; rehearse with a partner.','Demonstrates an interview answer with the host.','Give clearer, complete answers.','Worked demonstration, not an evaluation. For a simple numeric question a number may suffice; do not force an example or change the question to evade it.'),
821: ('Tristan de Montebello','L709–802; 01:23:05–01:37:10; L909–910','origin','Accordion: speak for successively shorter intervals, then expand while adding back only chosen material.','Coach describes client use; host reports it clarified his talk.','Clarify and internalize a talk.','Self-report and commercially promoted coaching method, not evidence of broad anxiety treatment or lasting skill gains. Script-free delivery may not suit accessibility needs or exact mandatory wording.'),
673: ('Jason Fried','L299–315; 00:38:09–00:41:08','support','Try a new way of working on a low-criticality project before applying it to important work.','Advice based on introducing Shape Up.','Create room to learn without making a first attempt decisive.','Pre-AI transfer idea, not tested AI adoption guidance. Low stakes alone does not provide useful feedback; define observable failure and a review point.'),
568: ('Bill Carr','L221–306; 00:35:26–00:50:27','origin','Write a customer/problem/solution proposal and FAQ; revise through widening circles of critique and allow ideas to be rejected.','Describes Amazon practice and consulting implementation.','Expose weak ideas and clarify customer value.','Retrospective field account, no causal comparison. Illustrative funnel counts are explicitly imaginary; the artifact is not evidence that customers want it. AI benefits would be an adaptation.'),
725: ('Maggie Crowley','L561–571; 01:12:37–01:14:13','origin','Write an outline, draw slide boxes, choose headline text, sketch visuals, then build the deck.','Reports one colleague found it much faster.','Clarify the argument before formatting.','Single anecdote; the reported time saving is not a benchmark. Paper is not essential where another accessible medium preserves author control.'),
737: ('Matthew Dicks','L440–510; 01:06:37–01:18:39','origin','Homework for Life: date and briefly record a lived moment; distinguish recalled past events from today’s events.','Reports collecting more moments over years and using some in stories.','Notice experience and build a personal story archive.','Self-reported frequency and coaching testimonials; no memory or therapeutic evaluation. Interpretations of others’ motives are not facts. Do not turn private experiences into public material without judgment.'),
601: ('Dan Hockenmaier','L164–210; 00:11:19–00:17:04','origin','Build a simple business model yourself with a knowledgeable analyst; expose assumptions and revise against results.','Reports building models at companies and learning how retention affects the system.','Develop understanding of drivers and trade-offs.','Pre-AI field account. Pain is not proof of learning; assumption stacking can produce misleading precision. No independent capability comparison.'),
660: ('Ian McAllister','L164–180; 00:21:14–00:23:04','support','After communicating, ask how you could answer better; the host suggests manager feedback and McAllister agrees.','Offers a self-review routine.','Improve future explanations.','No outcome data or scoring standard; a self-grade alone cannot show improvement. Supports rehearsal debrief rather than a separate automated-score practice.'),
677: ('Jeff Weinstein','L790–803; 02:30:29–02:33:22','support','Writing his first business review forced a newcomer to investigate the domain and express his own perspective.','Reports rewriting after feedback that the draft did not sound like him.','Learn through constructing an accountable explanation.','One retrospective story with an unusually demanding role; no evidence this pressure or timetable fits everyone. Supports writing-to-learn, not required imitation of Stripe onboarding.'),
702: ('Katie Dill','L265–379; 00:29:28–00:48:43','origin','Walk a real user journey as a multidisciplinary team; log friction, apply an explicit qualitative rubric and discuss scores.','Describes Stripe’s review process and an invoice-interface fix associated with reduced support need.','Build shared quality judgment and identify issues.','Self-reported field use, no controlled attribution. Scores are explicitly qualitative. Internal taste supplements, not replaces, user research and actual outcomes.'),
755: ('Nancy Duarte','L148–188; 00:16:22–00:22:56','origin','Listen through interviews/surveys before an internal talk; compare needs with the requested change and rehearse an ugly rough cut with leaders.','Describes annual and quarterly internal communication practice.','Make the message responsive to the audience’s situation.','First-person company account, no measured comprehension or behavioral change. Leaders may not represent the whole audience; psychological and biographical causal claims excluded.'),
}

# Each card: origin id, slug, title, recognizable situation, steps,
# editorial adaptation, what to notice. The adaptation is explicitly untested.
CARDS = [
(435,'check-missing-perspectives','Check missing perspectives against real evidence','An AI summary treats a varied group as if everyone had the same circumstances.', ['Read the answer and name a person or situation it may overlook.','Ask AI who might be disadvantaged or missing; treat its suggestions as leads.','Consult an actual source, lived account or affected person.','Revise only where that evidence warrants it.'], 'Keep an omission that is justified by scope distinct from one that distorts the answer.', 'Which specific outside evidence changed the answer?'),
(802,'seek-contrary-customer-evidence','Seek contrary evidence in customer material','You have a favored strategy and actual interviews or feedback you can use.', ['State your strategy and identify the customer records being compared.','Ask AI where the records conflict with the strategy, not just where they agree.','Read the original passages and, where needed, follow up with customers.','Decide whether to revise the strategy or investigate further.'], 'Require locators for every claimed contradiction and retain uncertainty; compare with an alternative only when useful.', 'Did a checked contradiction change your choice, or only produce a more persuasive story?'),
(760,'review-survey-questions-for-decisions','Review survey questions against the decision','A survey must yield interpretable answers rather than a number that looks useful.', ['Write the decision the answers should inform.','Ask a survey specialist or AI to critique each item, including bundled questions.','Split ambiguous items and check what each possible answer would mean.','Remove items that cannot inform an action or answer a relevant question.'], 'Try revised wording with a real respondent; AI approval is only a suggestion.', 'Can you explain what you would do differently for each answer?'),
(421,'make-ai-disclosure-reciprocal','Make AI disclosure specific and reciprocal','You use AI in work judged by others or write a policy governing their use.', ['Name the tools, the tasks they assisted and the tasks you performed.','State what you checked and who owns the final judgments and errors.','Make that statement available to the affected audience.','Apply the same expectations to policy authors; where appropriate, involve affected people in writing the policy.'], 'Use safe representative channels where challenging a policy carries a power risk; review the statement when the workflow changes.', 'Can someone tell which work and judgments were actually yours?'),
(529,'keep-a-writing-process-record','Keep a record of how the writing developed','You want an inspectable account of assistance, research and editorial choices.', ['Keep version history and dated early drafts while writing.','Retain raw notes, outlines and the research trail.','For each AI session, record date, tool, task, what you accepted and what you rejected.','Ask collaborators to describe their contribution and assistance; make your public statement match the record.'], 'Keep a proportionate record inside authorized storage; distinguish documentation from proof or a legal guarantee.', 'Could you reconstruct one important change without relying on memory?'),
(819,'explain-the-algorithm-you-own','Explain the algorithm you are responsible for','You own a product whose algorithm shapes people’s choices.', ['Write down the objective the algorithm is meant to optimize.','Identify its inputs and the data collection or tuning choices that matter.','Discuss the description with the people implementing it and resolve what you do not know.','Use that understanding in product review and investment decisions.'], 'Record intended objective separately from observed behavior and test cases. For third-party tools, record unknowns instead of inventing internals.', 'Can the accountable product owner explain an important trade-off and show how it is checked?'),
(436,'ask-before-workplace-data-disclosure','Ask how workplace disclosures will be used','An onboarding or workplace assistant asks for personal or contextual information.', ['Ask what additional data is collected beyond your answer.','Ask who can access it, whether it leaves the organization and how it is used in assessment.','Consider whether knowing the destination would change your answer.','Seek answers from an accountable owner or actual policy before making the disclosure decision.'], 'The final verification step narrows the source’s advice: a generated reply cannot certify a vendor’s actual practices.', 'Which questions received a documented answer, and which remain unknown?'),
(440,'record-human-interventions','Record the human work needed around AI','Important corrections or contextual decisions disappear from the visible output.', ['Keep a private dated note when you catch an error, intervene or give contextual advice.','Record the action and what happened next.','Use the record when discussing the work required, with relevant people.'], 'Separate observed result from estimated damage avoided or costs saved. Do not put confidential client details in an unauthorized log.', 'What types of intervention recur, and what would let the team detect them earlier?'),
(533,'ask-contextual-human-advisers','Ask advisers who know the subject and you','Generic advice leaves you uncertain about a real choice.', ['Form a specific question and ask for reasons, keeping the request light.','Choose some people with subject expertise and some who know your circumstances.','Look for surprises, disagreement and missing context across replies.','Make your own decision, thank contributors and report back on the result.'], 'Use neutral wording rather than asking others to approve your preferred plan. AI may help edit the question, but cannot supply those people’s actual judgments.', 'What did a person know that was absent from the initial framing?'),
(705,'argue-the-other-side-before-deciding','Explain the other side before deciding','A team is stuck between competing priorities.', ['Ask each side for the strongest reasons supporting the other option.','Check that the opposing side recognizes its position in that account.','Clarify the decision maker and deadline.','Decide after the discussion and make the rationale visible.'], 'The recognition check and written rationale are curator additions. An AI role-play can prepare you, but does not replace hearing the actual parties.', 'Did the discussion reveal a real trade-off or merely produce agreement?'),
(803,'practice-choosing-decisive-questions','Practice choosing the questions that change the plan','You generate many questions but struggle to identify which answers matter most.', ['Choose a low-stakes fictional scenario, such as bringing a teleportation device to market.','Generate the questions you want answered.','Limit yourself to two and explain which later decisions each answer changes.','Compare possible answers and the different plans they imply.'], 'Use a partner or AI to vary the scenario only after your attempt. Do not carry the artificial two-question limit into real safety or due-diligence work.', 'Can you show the downstream choice changed by each question?'),
(585,'rehearse-with-audience-experience','Rehearse with someone who knows the audience','An important presentation must withstand questions from particular people.', ['Run the presentation with a colleague who knows those people.','Have them anticipate the questions and concerns each audience member may raise.','Revise the explanation and prepare the evidence needed.','Where appropriate, meet actual participants before the main meeting to hear concerns.'], 'After the real meeting, compare anticipated with actual questions. AI rehearsal may generate possibilities, not knowledge of those individuals.', 'Which question exposed a gap in understanding rather than presentation polish?'),
(731,'answer-example-relevance','Answer, example, relevance','You need to answer a substantive question clearly and explain why the answer matters.', ['Listen to the actual question.','Give a direct answer.','Add a concrete example and describe its relevance to the listener.','Practice with another person and ask whether you answered their question.'], 'Use AI only as an optional question generator; do the answering yourself. Keep short factual questions short.', 'Did the example clarify the answer, or distract from an unanswered question?'),
(821,'accordion-speaking-rehearsal','Rehearse a talk by compressing and expanding it','You know the topic but the talk is cluttered or hard to deliver without a script.', ['Speak a rough three-minute version.','Reflect on what mattered, then repeat in two minutes, one minute and thirty seconds.','Repeat the short version, then expand back through one, two and three minutes.','Add back only supporting material you choose; keep a few main-point cues and rehearse with a person.'], 'The times are the author’s examples, not a validated dose. Preserve any needed accessibility support or mandatory wording.', 'What stayed essential across versions, and can a listener state it back?'),
(568,'write-a-proposal-to-test-the-idea','Write a proposal to test the idea','A product idea sounds plausible but its customer, problem or solution is still vague.', ['Write a plain customer/problem/solution statement and the important questions it raises.','Use evidence to make the customer and problem specific.','Review first with a small group, then wider groups where the idea warrants it.','Revise, compare alternatives or abandon the idea when the writing and critique expose weaknesses.'], 'If AI assists, retain responsibility for the initial explanation, evidence and acceptance of changes. AI assistance is not evaluated in this pre-AI account.', 'What did making the document reveal that a finished template would have hidden?'),
(601,'build-the-model-to-understand-it','Build the explanatory model yourself','A ready-made model gives outputs you cannot yet explain.', ['Start with a small conceptual model of the system.','Build the relevant team-level drivers with an analyst or knowledgeable partner.','Make the assumptions and trade-offs explicit.','Compare model expectations with actual results and revise over time.'], 'AI can assist calculations or ask questions after you form the relationships; check its work. This AI use is a transfer hypothesis.', 'Can you explain why changing one assumption changes the result?'),
(725,'outline-the-argument-before-slides','Outline the argument before making slides','Deck formatting is consuming attention before the message is clear.', ['Write the argument as an outline.','Sketch boxes for slides and write their headline text.','Check that the headlines form a coherent sequence.','Sketch supporting visuals, then build the slides.'], 'Use an accessible low-fidelity medium. If AI later formats slides, compare them with the argument you chose.', 'Did a headline or transition reveal a missing reason or unsupported claim?'),
(737,'record-lived-moments','Record a lived moment each day','You want material drawn from your experience rather than generated personal stories.', ['Briefly note a moment that caught your attention, with its date.','Record during the day or reflect at its end; a few words are enough.','Mark older memories as memories so they do not appear to have happened that day.','Return to the notes and choose which, if any, are worth developing into stories.'], 'Keep observations separate from interpretations of other people. AI may help search the archive later, but must not invent your experiences.', 'Which details would you otherwise have forgotten, and which interpretations need checking?'),
(702,'review-quality-through-a-real-journey','Review quality through a real user journey','Separate good-looking parts may combine into a confusing experience.', ['Choose an important end-to-end user journey and its responsible people.','Walk through it together across product, design and engineering.','Record friction and examples; assess them against explicit qualitative criteria.','Discuss differing judgments, decide fixes and revisit the experience.'], 'For AI-assisted artifacts, adapt the journey to the real reader or user task. This transfer has not been evaluated in the source.', 'Which disagreement changed the quality criterion or uncovered something a score concealed?'),
(755,'listen-before-shaping-the-message','Listen before shaping the message','You are about to ask an audience to change priorities or behavior.', ['Hear their situation through interviews or surveys.','Compare what you hear with what the presentation will ask them to do.','Rehearse a rough version with relevant people before polishing slides.','Revise the message using their feedback, then deliver it.'], 'Include affected people beyond the leadership layer where possible. Generated personas can suggest questions, not replace actual listening.', 'What changed because you heard from the audience rather than imagined them?'),
]

SUPPORT = {435:[491],802:[591],436:[482],585:[660,673],568:[677]}
RELATED = {421:['cheong-penalizing-transparency-2025'],441:['ibrahim-warmth-sycophancy-2026'],486:['nisbett-wilson-introspection-1977'],478:['ibrahim-warmth-sycophancy-2026']}

def write(p, text):
    p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    p.write_text(text.rstrip()+'\n', encoding='utf-8')

def citation(i):
    r=S[i]; return f'{DATA[i][0]} ({r.get("date") or "date unverified"}). {r["title"]}. {r["collection"]}.'

def fm(i, source=False):
    return '---\nstatus: emerging\narea: [preservation]\n'+('type: '+('talk' if S[i]['collection']=="Lenny\'s Podcast" else 'article')+'\n' if source else '')+'sources:\n  - '+json.dumps(citation(i),ensure_ascii=False)+'\n---\n\n'

def main():
    files={i:[] for i in DATA}
    for i,slug,title,use,steps,adapt,notice in CARDS:
        r=S[i]; d=DATA[i]; path=Path(r['workbench'])/'drafts/practices'/f'{slug}.md'
        supporting='\n'.join(f'- {citation(j)} Locators: {DATA[j][1]}. Adds: {DATA[j][3]} Limits: {DATA[j][6]}' for j in SUPPORT.get(i,[]))
        related='\n'.join(f'- [[{x}]] — related mechanism or tension; does not validate this routine.' for x in RELATED.get(i,[])) or 'No research claim attached to this draft. Source links below retain provenance.'
        body=fm(i)+f'# {title}\n\n## Use When\n\n{use}\n\n## Try It\n\n'+'\n'.join(f'{n}. {s}' for n,s in enumerate(steps,1))+f'\n\n**[Inference] Editorial adaptation / boundary:** {adapt}\n\n## Origin\n\n{citation(i)} Locators: {d[1]}. Editorial practice title'+(' (source names the routine).' if i in [533,731,821,737] else '.')+f' [Retained source](../../source.md).\n\n'+(f'Additional source support (not independent validation):\n\n{supporting}\n\n' if supporting else '')+f'## Evidence and Rationale\n\n**Basis:** '+('First-person practitioner interview and advice; relevant exchange read, not the entire interview.' if r['collection']=="Lenny's Podcast" else 'Available article body read; proposal and/or author demonstration, not direct evaluation of this routine.')+f'\n\n**Observed or reported:** {d[4]}\n\n**Intended:** {d[5]}\n\n**Related research:** No direct evaluation of this exact routine identified in this reading. Related entries below are context, not validation.\n\n**Untested:** Causal effects on decision quality, retained unaided capability, confidence calibration and long-term use. A better artifact or a confident report cannot establish these.\n\n## Limits\n\n{d[6]}\n\n## What to Notice\n\n[Inference] {notice} This is a proposed observation, not a validated measure.\n\n## Related\n\n{related}\n\n## Review Status\n\nDraft only. Stage 4 critique recorded; integration decision pending.\n'
        write(ROOT/path,body); files[i].append(path.as_posix())
        for j in SUPPORT.get(i,[]): files[j].append(path.as_posix())
    for i,d in DATA.items():
        r=S[i]; wb=ROOT/r['workbench']; author,loc,role,contribution,reported,proposed,limits=d
        podcast=r['collection']=="Lenny's Podcast"
        coverage=f'Read relevant complete transcript exchanges: {loc}. Other interview sections not reviewed; source audio not checked.' if podcast else 'Read the complete available local article body; linked images, videos and external claims not independently inspected.'
        if i==316: coverage+=' Available text ends at a paid-video invitation; that demonstration was not accessed.'
        decision='retain_counterexample_only' if role=='counterexample' else ('merge_as_support_with_limits' if role=='support' else 'draft_with_limits')
        review=dict(id=i,group_index=r['group_index'],decision=decision,coverage=coverage,locators=[loc],basis='Practitioner account / proposed exercise; no direct causal evaluation of the extracted practice.',reported_outcomes=[reported],proposed_outcomes=[proposed],limits=[limits],related_entries=RELATED.get(i,[]),practice_files=files[i],role=role,review_note=contribution,author=author,integration_decision='pending',draft_only=True)
        write(wb/'practice-review.json',json.dumps(review,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False))
        write(wb/'triage.md',f'# Practice triage — {r["title"]}\n\nDecision: {decision}; role: {role}.\n\n{contribution}\n\n## Scope\n\n{coverage}\n\n## Attribution and trust\n\n{author}; retained publication: {r["collection"]}, {r.get("date")}. Practitioner expertise supports relevance; it does not establish the claim’s truth. Publication reach is not evidence of effectiveness.\n\n## Decision\n\nProceed to bounded draft and critique only. Integration pending.')
        write(wb/'distill.md',f'# Practice distillation\n\n## Contribution\n\n{contribution}\n\n## Locators\n\n{loc}\n\n## Reported\n\n{reported}\n\n## Intended\n\n{proposed}\n\n## Excluded claims and limits\n\n{limits}\n\n## Drafts\n\n'+('\n'.join(f'- `{p}`' for p in files[i]) or 'No recommended practice: retain as a cautionary source note.')+'\n\n## Reading coverage\n\n'+coverage)
        write(wb/'critique.md',f'# Three-lens practice critique\n\n## Evidence lens\n\n{reported} This supports a bounded account of what the source reports, not causal effectiveness.\n\n## Practitioner lens\n\n{contribution} '+('The original framing is unsuitable as a recommended routine.' if role=='counterexample' else 'Steps are inspectable; any AI transfer and additional checks are labeled editorial in the draft.')+f'\n\n## Adversarial lens\n\n{limits}\n\n## Decision\n\n{decision}. Draft-only review complete. No integration approval.\n\n## Coverage\n\n{coverage}')
        rel='\n'.join(f'- [[{x}]] — comparison context, not a direct test of this source.' for x in RELATED.get(i,[])) or 'No canonical research connection asserted.'
        write(wb/'drafts/source.md',fm(i,True)+f'# {r["title"]}\n\n## Citation\n\n{citation(i)}\n\n[Retained original](../source.md).\n\n## Type\n\n'+('Practitioner interview transcript' if podcast else 'Practitioner newsletter article')+f'\n\n## Key Insight\n\n{contribution}\n\n## Key Findings\n\n- **Reported:** {reported}\n- **Proposed benefit:** {proposed}\n- **Locators:** {loc}\n\nThese are paraphrases of the retained source, not independently verified findings.\n\n## Relevance\n\nRole: {role}. Decision: {decision}.\n\n## Supports\n\n{rel}\n\n## Contradicts / Extends\n\n{limits}\n\n## Open Questions\n\nDoes this routine improve the intended outcome under a comparison that separates artifact quality, unaided capability and felt confidence?\n\n## Reading Coverage\n\n{coverage}\n\n## Decision\n\nDraft only; integration pending.')
        meta=json.loads((wb/'source.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig'));meta.update(status='critiqued',draft_only=True,integration_decision='pending',practice_review='practice-review.json');write(wb/'source.json',json.dumps(meta,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False))
        with (wb/'log.md').open('a',encoding='utf-8') as f:f.write(f'\n## 2026-09-26 — practice close reading\n\n{decision}. {coverage} Distillation and three-lens critique recorded. Integration decision pending.\n')
    write(BASE/'root-review-record.json',json.dumps({'source_ids':sorted(DATA),'practice_count':len(CARDS),'draft_only':True},indent=2))
    print(f'Reviewed sources: {len(DATA)}; origin practice drafts: {len(CARDS)}; no integration.')

if __name__=='__main__':main()
